 on the answers.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. **`docs/proposal.md`**: change exactly these paragraphs.\n   - **L89** (Claims need a single owner): replace \"a Kubernetes Lease in-cluster\" with \"`flock` in both modes. In-cluster the lock file is on the `-home` PVC, which is `ReadWriteOncePod`, and the Deployment has `replicas: 1` and the `Recreate` strategy (decision 9)\".\n   - **L93** (Retries): keep \"node eviction under Kubernetes\" and name its cause `evicted` (transient; gap 4).\n   - **L288-L292**, the table's Kubernetes column:\n     - Workspace: \"the same worktree as local, packed into a tarball the pod fetches from the job API into an `emptyDir` with `sizeLimit`; the result is pushed back as a full tree\".\n     - Runtime: \"a bare Pod (no Job object): init `fetch`, agent container under `ard job-exec`, native sidecar `upload`; default `ard-agent` image; hard timeout\".\n     - Heartbeat: \"the controller follows `pods/log` into the host log; log mtime\".\n     - Logs: \"followed into `~/.ard/logs/...`; stdout from the uploaded tee file\".\n     - Labels: unchanged.\n   - **New paragraph after L293**, \"Kubernetes-executor details\". It covers the flow, decisions 1 to 8 and 10 to 12, the pod paths (`/job`, `/work`, `/role`, `/sidecar`, `/ard-config`, `/ard-out`, `/ard-bin`), the deadline arithmetic (gap 13), the result layout (`work/` and `out/stdout`, gap 2), and that the executor is not an effect.\n   - **L312**: replace \"the Kubernetes `Job` gets a short TTL\" with \"a Kubernetes job's pod and per-job Secret are deleted (no Job object, no TTL)\".\n   - **L409**: under `executor: k8s` every role runs the default `ard-agent` image and a role `Dockerfile` is ignored until in-cluster builds land. Name the affected starter roles (gap 7).\n   - **L414**: `executor: local # local | k8s; fixed at startup` (gap 6). Add one sentence after the yaml block saying the cluster settings are `ard serve` flags set by the chart.\n   - **Guardrails L460**: replace \"a network policy limits egress to the model API and package registries\" with \"job egress is DNS, the job API, and the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254\". Add four bullets after it:\n     - **Job API key** (decision 4).\n     - **Job API listener** (decision 7 plus the TLS choice).\n     - **Result untar is the boundary** (decision 6).\n     - **Cluster** (decision 10) with residual risks: the model credential sits in a per-job Secret in etcd; the tee'd stdout is agent-writable (gap 1); pod egress reaches the whole internet; the Pod Security `restricted` profile relies on the cluster's admission.\n   - **L520**: replace \"with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock`\" with \"with the k8s executor and the same `flock` on a `ReadWriteOncePod` volume\".\n   - **Open questions**: add a checked entry, \"[x] Kubernetes executor (#54): decided\", linking the design comment and #312.\n2. **The TLS choice** is recorded in the Guardrails \"Job API listener\" bullet and the details paragraph. The options for the owner:\n\n   | Option | What | For | Against |\n   | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n   | **A (recommended)** | ECDSA P-256 self-signed cert generated in memory at `ard serve` start, SAN = the Service DNS name. The SHA-256 of the leaf DER goes into each per-job Secret (`fingerprint`), and `job-fetch`/`job-upload` pin it with `VerifyConnection` | No CA or cert-manager; restart rotates the cert, matching keys that already die on restart; protects key and result on a CNI with no encryption | ~120 lines in jobapi; the pin must be in the Secret before the pod starts (it is) |\n   | B | Plain HTTP behind NetworkPolicy (architect) | Simplest | The key and the result tarball cross the pod network in clear; anything on the node path can read the key or tamper with the result before the controller sees it |\n   | C | cert-manager `Certificate`, CA bundle in the Secret | Standard rotation | New cluster dependency and chart work in #319; more moving parts |\n   | D | Mesh mTLS | Transparent | Out of scope for one controller |\n\n   Part 4 implements A behind `--job-api-tls=self-signed|off`, with `off` refused unless dry-run, if the owner picks A. B needs only the `off` path.\n3. No change to `docs/layout.md` (no package added).\n\n### Test plan\n\nNo Go code. `make lint test` must stay green. Checks the reviewer runs:\n- `grep -n \"Lease\\|short TTL\\|Kubernetes \\`Job\\`\" docs/proposal.md` returns nothing.\n- Every new link resolves to #54, #312 or the design comment.\n- The Executors table still has 3 columns on every row.\n\n### Risk\n\nLow. Docs only. The risk is drift: parts 2 to 7 are planned against these answers (TLS A, stdout from the tee file, executor fixed at startup, `evicted` cause), so a different owner answer means re-planning parts 4, 6 and 7 before they start.\n\n### Size\n\nS, about 100 to 140 changed lines in `docs/proposal.md`.\n\n\n### albertcbrown at 2026-11-24T12:58:59Z\n\nOwner decisions (2026-11-24) for the #54 decision record:\n1. **TLS:** option A. `ard serve` generates a self-signed ECDSA cert at startup; its SHA-256 fingerprint is pinned in each per-job Secret.\n2. **Upload:** a **plain second container** (waits for the done marker, uploads, exits), not a native sidecar. No Kubernetes 1.29+ minimum for this.\n3. **Stdout:** from the `job-exec` tee file shipped in the result, at `out/stdout` outside `work/`. Accepted risk, recorded in the ADR: the agent can rewrite it and so forge its own usage figures, the same exposure local Docker has today. Exit code still comes from the kubelet.\n4. **Agent image:** published **publicly** as part of ard under `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard`. It must contain no secrets (no credentials, tokens or keys baked in; a test checks the image). #243's \"never published\" header changes accordingly in #320.\n5. **Cluster settings** (jobs namespace, API address, images) come from `ard serve` flags set by the chart, not `.ard/config.yaml` (devops and tech lead agree; repo content cannot pick a namespace).\n6. #317 is about 2,000 lines: split it at planning time into the API layer (types, REST client, fake) and the executor.\n\n### ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot] at 2026-11-24T13:37:07Z\n\n<!-- ard:closeout job=01M86WBQW1HJ1RA05YF557CEJE attempt=1 -->\n**ard · triage:lead · done**\n\nThis is a docs-only change: it records the approved Kubernetes executor design as a decision and updates the matching sections of docs/proposal.md. No code changes. The scope is clear and the owner has answered the open questions in the comments, so the engineer plans the edits and the engineer worker makes them.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: turn the comment plan and the owner's decisions into exact edit steps for docs/proposal.md, and match them to the file's current line numbers.\n- worker engineer: edit the documentation (decision record, Executors table, cleanup text, lock mentions, Guardrails).\n\n## Open issues\n- The design comment on the parent issue holds decisions 1 to 12, and a gaps file is also referenced. Neither is in the repository, and the worker cannot fetch them. If the engineer cannot rebuild every decision from the issue comments, a maintainer should paste the decision text into the issue or the repository.\n- The comment plan describes the upload step as a native sidecar, but the owner's later decision chose a plain second container. The plan should follow the owner's decision.\n- The line numbers cited for docs/proposal.md have drifted. For example, the Job TTL text is now near line 317 and the second Lease mention near line 544.\n- No architect, product-manager or documentation-planner triage roles exist, so only the engineer plans this. The owner already approved the design.\n\nRequested next: `ard:triage:engineer`\nOutput: [.ard/312/triage-lead.md](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/8aa3a491b64231eff7fdec1a8d72bf37790674de/.ard/312/triage-lead.md) · log [.ard/312/logs/triage-lead-20261124T133642.277Z.log](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/8aa3a491b64231eff7fdec1a8d72bf37790674de/.ard/312/logs/triage-lead-20261124T133642.277Z.log) · job `01M86WBQW1HJ1RA05YF557CEJE` · attempt 1\n\n\n### ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot] at 2026-11-24T13:52:09Z\n\n<!-- ard:closeout job=01M86X7ANBJSSCJD9H02APVS95 attempt=1 -->\n**ard · triage:engineer · needs-human**\n\nNo plan was written. The change is docs-only (size S, risk low), but the text of the design decisions it must record is not in the repository, so a worker could only guess at it.\n\n## Open issues\n- The design comment's decisions 1 to 12 and the gaps file are not in the repo or the issue comments. The comments only name decisions 4, 6, 7, 9 and 10 by topic, and the owner's later answers cover TLS, upload, stdout, image, settings and the split of #317. Decisions 1 to 3, 5, 8, 11 and 12, the pod and deadline details (gap 13) and gaps 1, 2, 4, 6 and 7 are missing. A maintainer should paste the full design text into the issue, or commit it under docs/, so the worker can copy it faithfully.\n- The issue does not say where the decision record lives. docs/ has only proposal.md, layout.md, TODO.md and runbooks/, and no decision-record directory. Please name the file, or confirm that the proposal's Open questions entry is the record.\n- The earlier plan comment's upload step is a native sidecar, which the owner's later answer replaces with a plain second container that waits for the done marker. The table's Runtime cell and the details paragraph must follow the owner's answer.\n- The earlier plan's line numbers have drifted. Current positions in docs/proposal.md: the Lease mention is at line 89, retries at 93, the executors table at 291 to 297, Job TTL at 317, the Dockerfile bullet at 430, the config yaml at 435, the egress bullet at 484, the second Lease mention at 544, and Open questions at 556. The worker should match on text, not line number.\n- The owner-chosen answers the plan should use are TLS option A (self-signed ECDSA cert, fingerprint pinned in each per-job Secret), a plain upload container, stdout from the tee file at out/stdout as an accepted risk, a public agent image with no secrets, and cluster settings as ard serve flags.\n\nOutput: [.ard/312/triage-engineer.md](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/180b7bfcd746fa5c0fe98f104c1aa90f756d004c/.ard/312/triage-engineer.md) · log [.ard/312/logs/triage-engineer-20261124T135146.544Z.log](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/180b7bfcd746fa5c0fe98f104c1aa90f756d004c/.ard/312/logs/triage-engineer-20261124T135146.544Z.log) · job `01M86X7ANBJSSCJD9H02APVS95` · attempt 1\n\n\n### albertcbrown at 2026-11-24T13:52:29Z\n\n## Kubernetes executor design (owner-approved, 2026-11-24)\n\nReplaces the proposal's Kubernetes column (init-container clone into an emptyDir, pod-phase heartbeat, Job TTL) and the earlier NFS/RWX idea, which the owner rejected for metadata performance. Reviewed by devops, architect and security.\n\n**Flow.** `ard serve` prepares the job exactly as the local executor does (git worktree from the bare clone, scrub, home, config dir, untrusted inputs, sidecar, a read-only copy of the role dir), packs it into a tarball, mints a per-job key and starts a bare **Pod** (no Job object). The pod fetches its input from an API on `ard serve`, runs the agent, and uploads its result; the controller unpacks the result into a fresh directory that becomes `Handle.Worktree`, and close-out runs unchanged (`gitx.Snapshot` diffs any plain directory against `BaseSHA`).\n\n**Decisions**\n1. **Volume:** `emptyDir` with `sizeLimit` and matching `ephemeral-storage` request/limit. Nothing is shared between pods or with the controller. Eviction maps to a failed (transient) job.\n2. **Results:** the pod **pushes** a full-tree result (PUT). The controller unpacks into `<id>.result/`, swaps it in as the worktree (`<id>.input` kept until cleanup), writes `<id>.done`. Deletions are deletions in `Snapshot.Changed`. No diff from the pod; no git in the pod (`.git` is not shipped; `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false`, also for local Docker parity).\n3. **Pod shape and the key:** the key never enters the agent container.\n   - init container `fetch` (ard image, key mounted from a per-job Secret): downloads and safely unpacks the input, copies a static `ard` into a shared `emptyDir`;\n   - agent container: `/ard-bin/ard job-exec -- <agent cmd>`; tees stdout to a file, enforces a deadline slightly under the pod's, writes a done marker; no key mount, no SA token;\n   - native sidecar `upload` (key mounted): uploads once the marker appears, retries with backoff until the deadline.\n4. **Key:** 32 bytes from `crypto/rand`, base64url; only its SHA-256 kept, in memory; constant-time compare; bound to one job (a job id in the URL must match); downloads repeat until the first upload; exactly one successful upload, accepted only after the agent container is `Terminated`; revoked at upload, deadline plus grace, or cleanup; added to the job's redaction set by value. Not persisted: after a controller restart the Reaper kills orphan pods and a late upload gets 401.\n5. **Exit code, stdout and usage** come from the kubelet (`containerStatuses`) and the controller's own log follow, never from the tarball; a pod that ends without uploading is `Gone` (failure), never `Exited` 0.\n6. **Untar (both directions; the result side is the security boundary):** Go `archive/tar` through `os.Root` into an empty dir; only regular files, dirs and symlinks (created last); modes masked to 0644/0755; reject hardlinks, devices, FIFOs, absolute paths, `..`, NUL, duplicates, any `.git` component; cap compressed and decompressed bytes (counted on the stream), entry count, per-file size, path length/depth, gzip ratio. The input tarball stores symlinks as links (never followed) and contains only the job's roots.\n7. **Listener:** a separate `http.Server` on its own port with a private mux and exactly two routes (`GET /v1/jobs/{id}/input`, `PUT /v1/jobs/{id}/result`); auth before reading any body; header/idle timeouts, `MaxBytesReader`, connection cap, global rate limit on failed auth; never logs headers. Runs while paused. Not an effect (dry-run runs it as local jobs run today). **TLS: open, owner to confirm in part 1** (devops and security recommend a self-signed cert generated at startup with its fingerprint pinned in the per-job Secret; the architect accepts plain HTTP behind NetworkPolicy).\n8. **Logs and heartbeat:** the controller follows `pods/log` into `h.LogPath` on its own volume (heartbeat stays the controller's write); the teed stdout file in the result is the authoritative stdout. Events for FailedScheduling.\n9. **Leader:** no Lease. Keep `flock`; the `-home` PVC becomes `ReadWriteOncePod`; `replicas: 1`, Recreate.\n10. **Cluster:** jobs in their own namespace with Pod Security `restricted`; a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy refusing job pods that reference the App Secret or hostPath; job SA with no roles and no token; `enableServiceLinks: false`; non-root, drop ALL, no privilege escalation, RuntimeDefault seccomp, read-only root fs, `/tmp` on emptyDir; `restartPolicy: Never`; image pinned by digest; job egress: DNS, the ard-serve port, the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254; ard-serve ingress only from job pods on the API port. Controller Role (namespaced): pods create/get/list/watch/delete, pods/log get, secrets create/delete, events list; no pods/exec, no PVC rights. Per-job Secret owned by the Pod.\n11. **Client:** a thin REST client over the in-cluster SA token behind a consumer interface with a fake (no client-go), unless part 1 decides otherwise.\n12. **Images:** roles with a Dockerfile use the default `ard-agent` image until in-cluster builds exist (its own issue).\n\n\n### albertcbrown at 2026-11-24T13:52:31Z\n\n## Gaps found while planning (tech lead and devops, 2026-11-24)\n\nRecord each as decided (where the owner's answers below settle it) or as an Open question in the proposal.\n\n# #54 design gaps found while planning #312 to #318\n\nOrdered by impact. \"Settle in #312\" means the owner's answer goes into the decision record before parts 4 to 7 start.\n\n1. **Decisions 5 and 8 contradict each other on stdout (settle in #312).** Decision 5 says stdout and usage never come from the tarball. Decision 8 says the tee'd stdout file in the result is the authoritative stdout. `pods/log` merges stdout and stderr, so the adapter (claude stream-json parse, scanner findings via `Logs(Stdout)`) needs the tee file.\n   - The tee file sits in `/ard-out`, which is writable by the agent (same uid as `job-exec`). Agent Bash can therefore rewrite it to forge `total_cost_usd`, which undercounts budget, or a `rate_limit_event`, which pauses the account.\n   - The same is true today under local Docker: the agent can write to `/proc/1/fd/1` of the same-uid `docker-init`. So this is parity, not a regression.\n   - Recommendation: the exit code comes only from the kubelet. Stdout and usage come from the tee file, with the risk documented in Guardrails' residual risks. Revisit when `PodLogOptions.stream` (split stdout/stderr) is GA on the cluster.\n2. **The result layout was undefined.** The teed stdout must not be inside `work/`, or it would appear in `Snapshot.Changed`. The plans define it in `jobapi/contract.go` (#315): input roots `work/ role/ config/ sidecar/`; result roots `work/ out/` (`out/stdout`).\n3. **Native sidecar lifecycle.**\n   - When the agent container exits, kubelet SIGTERMs the `upload` sidecar. The upload must therefore finish within `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, which #317 must set to cover it (for example 120 s), and #316 must keep uploading after SIGTERM when the marker exists.\n   - A sidecar that exits on its own is restarted (`restartPolicy: Always`). So `job-upload` blocks after success until SIGTERM.\n   - The first upload attempts get 409 until the controller sees `Terminated`.\n   - Native sidecars need k8s ≥ 1.29 (GA 1.33). #319 sets `kubeVersion`.\n4. **Eviction has no transient cause.** `reaper.CauseOf` (finish.go:92-116) maps an in-process `Gone` to `no_process`, which is not retried. Decision 1 and proposal L93 say eviction is a transient failure. The plans add `executor.Status.Transient` (#317) and `closeout.CauseEvicted` plus the `CauseOf` case (#318). No part owned this.\n5. **\"Key added to the job's redaction set by value\" has no plumbing.** Redaction is built from `os.Environ()` (servecmd/worker.go:50, closeout/paths.go:87). The key never enters the agent container or the followed logs, it is single-use and job-bound, and it is revoked before close-out. Recommendation: drop the requirement, or have #318 merge `Keys` live values into the `Secrets` funcs (about 20 lines). Not planned by default.\n6. **Where the executor mode and cluster settings come from.**\n   - `.ard/config.yaml` is reloaded every tick from `origin/main`, but the executor is built once at startup (`wiring.newExecutor`), and startup may have no config.\n   - Namespace, API address, images and TLS are deployment settings that belong in the chart, not in a repo file.\n   - Recommendation (planned in #318): `executor:` is fixed at startup; the cluster settings are `ard serve` flags or env; a later config with a different `executor` is a config error (paused) until restart.\n   - Consequence: #318's \"ard config check prints the new keys\" becomes \"prints a note\"; there are no new config keys. If the owner wants a `k8s:` block in config.yaml instead, #318 grows by about 150 lines in `internal/config`.\n7. **Decision 12 breaks the starter roles that have Dockerfiles.** The scanners `cve`, `docs`, `error-codes`, `lint`, `missing-tests` and `trufflehog` (internal/roles/scanners/*/Dockerfile) and any worker whose `tests` need a toolchain would run on `ard-agent` without their tools. Either #320's `ard-agent` image carries those tools (golangci-lint, trufflehog, the cve tools, Go), or these roles stay off under k8s until in-cluster builds. The rollout must say which.\n8. **The diff guard counts moves twice.** `Snapshot.Numstat` uses `--no-renames` (gitx/snapshot.go:162), so a moved line counts as one deletion plus one addition.\n   - #313 comes to about 1,450 lines only if the behaviour tests stay in `local`.\n   - #317 is about 2,000 lines as one PR, so it should split into 317A (`kube/api`: types, REST client, fake; M) and 317B (executor; L).\n   - #314, #315 and #316 are each about 900 to 1,000 lines, which is L, not M.\n9. **The real REST client was unassigned.** #317's body names \"a thin REST client interface plus a fake\". The in-cluster implementation (projected token re-read, CA, no redirects) has to live somewhere; it is planned in 317A.\n10. **The env policy is in `local`.** `checkEnv`, `checkFixed`, `ardEnv` and `ghRE` (local/env.go) are needed by `kube`. They are planned into `executor.EnvPolicy` in #313, which is a small scope addition to that issue.\n11. **Moving the worktree breaks its git registration.** Swapping `<id>` to `<id>.input` moves a registered worktree, so `git worktree remove <id>` would hit the result dir. #317's Cleanup removes the dirs and then runs `git worktree prune`. An alternative is for kube Prepare to export the tree (`ExportTree`, as sidecars do) and never register a worktree.\n12. **Pod name length.** `Job.ID` allows 64 chars (executor/validate.go:15), but `ard-<id>` must be ≤ 63 (a DNS label). ULIDs and worker steps (`<ulid>t1`) fit; kube Prepare rejects anything over 59. Names and URLs use the lowercased id, and ULIDs are case-insensitive.\n13. **The deadline arithmetic was unspecified (settle in #312).** Pending and image-pull time count against the pod's `activeDeadlineSeconds`, and the Reaper kills owned jobs at `h.Deadline + 71s` (decide.go:15). Planned:\n    - `h.Deadline = Start + Timeout`;\n    - the job-exec deadline is `h.Deadline − min(2m, Timeout/10)`;\n    - pod `activeDeadlineSeconds = Timeout + UploadGrace` (2 min);\n    - the key is revoked at `Deadline + UploadGrace`;\n    - job-exec exits 124 at its deadline, which maps to `TimedOut`.\n14. **The exit code must outlive the pod.** After the pod is deleted, kubelet state is gone. The controller writes the kubelet's exit code into `<id>.done` at Accept, and Status reads it. Nothing in the tarball is trusted for it.\n15. **Handles from the other executor.** After an `executor:` switch across a restart, the running executor gets handles of the other `Kind`. Each executor must report a foreign-kind handle as `Gone` and limit Cleanup to removing directories. Leftover pods are bounded by `activeDeadlineSeconds` and by the #318 sweep.\n16. **Orphan pods with no state record** (a crash between `CreatePod` and `SaveHandle`) are not reached by the Reaper's Status path. Planned: `kube.Sweep` in the pool-adopt hook (#318), scoped by an `ard/repo` hash label so that two repos sharing the jobs namespace cannot delete each other's pods. #319's Role allows only `pods list` and `delete` in that namespace, which is enough.\n17. **The model credential lives in the per-job Secret** (etcd) so the agent container can get it via `secretKeyRef` without the key item. This is a residual risk for Guardrails: rely on the cluster's etcd encryption at rest. The fetch and upload containers mount only the `key` and `fingerprint` items.\n18. **`job-exec` is PID 1 in the agent container** and must reap orphans, which Docker's `--init` does locally. This is planned in #316 and is not in the design.\n19. **Input size limits** (large repos and sidecars) need defaults that agree with the `emptyDir` `sizeLimit`. #317 takes them from `tarball.Limits` plus `--job-resources`. #312 should name the defaults.\n\n\n### albertcbrown at 2026-11-24T13:52:32Z\n\nAnswers to the triage engineer's questions:\n- **Design text:** decisions 1-12 are in the design comment above (copied from #54); the gaps are in the comment after it.\n- **Where the record lives:** follow CLAUDE.md's decision-issue rule: the decision is recorded **as a comment on this issue** (the design comment plus the owner's answers) and in **docs/proposal.md**, which is the source of truth. No new decision-record file or directory. The proposal gets the Executors table and details, the Guardrails text, and an Open questions entry for each gap the owner has not settled.\n- **Owner answers override the earlier plan:** TLS option A; a **plain second upload container** (not a native sidecar), so the Runtime cell and details paragraph say so; stdout from the tee file at `out/stdout` as an accepted risk; the agent image public under `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard` with no secrets; cluster settings as `ard serve` flags; #317 split into the API layer and the executor.\n- **Egress (gap):** the approved design (decision 10) allows the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254, matching #276; replace the Guardrails text that says model API and package registries only.\n- Match proposal edits on text, not line numbers.\n\n### ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot] at 2026-11-24T14:09:10Z\n\n<!-- ard:closeout job=01M86Y5P4JPPZP6D9P25KM4W32 attempt=1 -->\n**ard · triage:engineer · done**\n\nPlans a docs-only edit of docs/proposal.md that records the approved Kubernetes executor design and the owner's answers: lock mentions, retries, the Executors table column, a new details paragraph, cleanup text, configuration notes, Guardrails and Open questions. Size S, risk low.\n\n## Open issues\n- The plan follows the owner's answers over the earlier plan comment: a plain second upload container, not a native sidecar.\n- The worker must match on text, not line numbers, because the cited lines have drifted.\n- Gaps 3, 5 and 11 are not settled by the owner, so the plan records them as unchecked Open questions.\n\nOutput: [.ard/312/triage-engineer.md](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/8dc617ea93a4f537791e61851bfbce802425b86f/.ard/312/triage-engineer.md) · log [.ard/312/logs/triage-engineer-20261124T140820.975Z.log](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/8dc617ea93a4f537791e61851bfbce802425b86f/.ard/312/logs/triage-engineer-20261124T140820.975Z.log) · job `01M86Y5P4JPPZP6D9P25KM4W32` · attempt 1\n\n\n### albertcbrown at 2026-11-24T17:27:17Z\n\nHold: the owner is revising the design (2026-11-24): pre and post sidecars that run ard's own code and hold the secrets, an isolated agent container, and **no job API on ard serve**. The tarball-API decisions (job API, per-job key, TLS) are under review again; do not start the worker on this until the revised design is posted here and the issue is re-approved.\n\n### albertcbrown at 2026-11-24T17:34:06Z\n\n## Kubernetes executor design, revision 3 (owner-approved 2026-11-24)\n\n**Supersedes** the tarball-API design (job API on `ard serve`, per-job key for it, TLS pinning). Reviewed by devops, architect and security. The owner chose: **pre and post sidecars running ard's own code**, an **isolated agent container**, **no API on `ard serve`**, and the result pulled by the controller from the **post container's API, secured by a preshared key unique to each agent pod**.\n\n### Pod shape (one bare Pod per job step, `restartPolicy: Never`, `emptyDir` volumes)\n1. **`pre` (init container, ard image):** prepares the workspace.\n   - Fetches the repo at `BaseSHA` (`git fetch --depth 1 origin <BaseSHA>`, checks `HEAD == BaseSHA`) with a **read-only installation token** the controller mints per job: `repositories: [job repo (+ sidecar repos in the same installation)]`, `permissions: {contents: read}`. History jobs fetch full history.\n   - The token comes from a per-job Secret mounted **only in `pre`**, passed to git as an `http.extraheader` from the environment (never argv, never `.git/config`). The git dir lives on a `ctl` emptyDir the agent does not mount; the agent's `work` volume gets the tree with no `.git`.\n   - Role dir: exported from the config commit (`cfg.SHA`) in the same fetch; k8s refuses a config without a SHA. Sidecars at their pinned SHAs (the controller resolves refs before the pod).\n   - Untrusted inputs and the scrub list: in the same per-job Secret (inputs capped at 768 KiB; over the cap is a permanent failure with a named cause).\n   - Runs the **same `workspace` code** as local `Prepare` (scrub, `rejectSymlink`, input writes via `os.Root`, home, config dir, sidecar), so the two cannot drift. Copies the static `ard` binary for the wrapper.\n   - The controller revokes the token (`DELETE /installation/token`) once `pre` has terminated. Tokens are added to the job's redaction set.\n2. **`agent` (the role image or `ard-agent`):** `/ard-bin/ard job-exec -- <agent cmd>`. Mounts only `work` (read-only for ReadOnly jobs) and an `out` volume. Holds **only its model credential** (`secretKeyRef`). No GitHub token, no key, no Secret volume, no SA token. Tees stdout to `out/stdout` (accepted risk,","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M87BBZVW7H66ZGHRQ6GCS2YM/d5921479-f508-4321-b4e7-b5e5335ff67e/tool-results/bnci4vkxu.txt","persistedOutputSize":48758},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012fv38c4zeSJhRPthxzqbDD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
